Serbia’s history spans medieval kingdoms, centuries under Ottoman rule, and its emergence as an independent state in the 19th century. The medieval Serbian state reached its height under Stefan Dušan in the 14th century, before its defeat by the Ottomans at the Battle of Kosovo in 1389. Serbia gradually won autonomy and independence from the Ottoman Empire during the 1800s.

After World War I, Serbia became part of the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, later renamed Yugoslavia. Serbia was a central republic in socialist Yugoslavia after World War II. As Yugoslavia broke apart in the 1990s, Serbia was involved in regional conflicts and later formed a union with Montenegro. Serbia became an independent country again in 2006. Its modern history is also closely connected with Kosovo, which declared independence in 2008;
